            <ArticleTitle>Small-bowel neuroendocrine tumor and retroperitoneal fibrosis: efficacy of octreotide and tamoxifen.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e24-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5301/tj.5000259</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">9BA35EBE-8879-4961-BC86-DC58FB506C01</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS AND BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Neuroendocrine tumors are uncommon clinical entities and only a few cases of the co-occurrence of neuroendocrine tumors and retroperitoneal fibrosis have been described in the literature.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS AND STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report the promising results achieved in a case of neuroendocrine tumor complicated by retroperitoneal fibrosis causing right ureteral obstruction treated with long-acting release octreotide and tamoxifen.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS AND CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The treatment resulted in a complete response without toxicity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
